---
import GuideLayout from '../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
    {
        question: 'How do I turn a company announcement into a blog post?',
        answer:
            'Paste the announcement text into PressFolio, run the auto-parse to extract the source, date, headline, quote, and figures, refine anything that reads wrong, then generate the post and export it as Markdown or HTML.',
    },
    {
        question: 'Which kinds of announcements work best?',
        answer:
            'Funding rounds, product launches, partnerships, hires, awards, and policy or regulatory announcements all convert well because they share a predictable structure: a headline claim, a supporting quote, and a few key numbers.',
    },
    {
        question: 'Should I publish the announcement as-is or rewrite it?',
        answer:
            'Publish a converted article, not the raw announcement. Announcements are written for journalists and open with a dateline, while a blog post needs a reader-facing opening and context for people who have not been following the story.',
    },
];
---

<GuideLayout
    eyebrow="Use case"
    heading="Announcement to Blog Post: Funding, Product, and Hiring News"
    metaTitle="Announcement to Blog Post"
    intro="Announcements are written for press distribution, not for readers. Here is how to convert funding rounds, product launches, partnerships, and hiring news into blog posts that people actually finish reading."
    description="Convert funding rounds, product launches, partnerships, and hiring news into blog posts readers finish, without losing a figure or a quote."
    breadcrumb={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Announcement to Blog', path: '/announcement-to-blog' }]}
    faqs={faqs}
>
    <h2>Why raw announcements do not work as blog posts</h2>
    <p>
        An announcement opens with a dateline and a city, states the news in the third person, and attributes a quote
        to someone the reader may not know. That structure exists to make a journalist’s job easy. On your own blog it
        reads as distant and gets abandoned in the first paragraph.
    </p>
    <p>
        The fix is not to invent a story. It is to reorder the same facts: lead with the thing that changed, explain
        why it matters to the reader, keep the quote as supporting evidence, and put the figures in a short list
        instead of burying them in prose.
    </p>

    <h2>Converting each announcement type</h2>
    <h3>Funding and investment announcements</h3>
    <p>
        Lead with the amount, the round, and the investor. Then explain what the money funds and over what period. The
        figures to preserve are the round size, the valuation if disclosed, the total raised to date, and the stated
        use of funds. Avoid repeating the boilerplate “about the company” paragraph at the end — move anything
        genuinely useful into the body.
    </p>

    <h3>Product and feature launches</h3>
    <p>
        Lead with the problem the product solves, not the version number. Keep availability, pricing, and platform
        support as explicit facts. Product announcements degrade badly when the copy drifts into adjectives, so hold
        the feature list to what the release actually claims.
    </p>

    <h3>Partnerships and integrations</h3>
    <p>
        State who is partnering with whom in the first sentence, then explain what the customer gets. Preserve both
        companies’ names in full on first mention, and keep any quoted spokespeople attributed with their titles.
    </p>

    <h3>Hires, promotions, and awards</h3>
    <p>
        These are short by nature. Lead with the name, the role, and the remit. Keep the previous role and any
        relevant numbers — years of experience, team size, revenue owned — as supporting detail rather than the
        headline.
    </p>

    <h2>What to keep, what to drop</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Keep</th><th>Drop or move</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>The specific change announced</td><td>Datelines and “FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE” headers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Figures exactly as issued</td><td>Boilerplate “about us” paragraphs</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Attributed quotes and names</td><td>Media contact blocks</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dates, availability, pricing</td><td>Repeated superlatives and unverifiable claims</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Then publish it properly</h2>
    <p>
        A converted post still needs a title tag, a meta description, and accurate attribution. Work through the
        <a href="/blog/press-release-seo-checklist">press release SEO checklist</a> before publishing, and if you are
        producing multiple formats from the same announcement, see
        <a href="/pr-content-repurposing">PR content repurposing</a>. To run the conversion now, use the
        <a href="/">PressFolio converter</a>.
    </p>
</GuideLayout>